ignant pleuritis</article-title><source>Eur J Cancer</source><year>2006</year><volume>42</volume><issue>14</issue><fpage>2382</fpage><lpage>2389</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">16899364</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R26"><label>26</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Kozawa</surname><given-names>O</given-names></name><name><surname>Suzuki</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Tokuda</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Kotoyori</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Ito</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><name><surname>Oiso</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Effect of retinoic acid on prostaglandin F2 alpha-induced prostaglandin E2 synthesis in osteoblast-like cells</article-title><source>Horm Metab Res</source><year>1994</year><volume>26</volume><issue>8</issue><fpage>374</fpage><lpage>378</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">7806133</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="R27"><label>27</label><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yamamoto</surname><given-names>E</given-names></name><name><surname>Kohama</surname><given-names>G</given-names></name><name><surname>Sunakawa</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name><name><surname>Iwai</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Hiratsuka</surname><given-names>H</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Mode of invasion, bleomycin sensitivity, and clinical course in squamous cell carcinoma of the oral cavity</article-title><source>Cancer</source><year>1983</year><volume>51</volume><issue>12</issue><fpage>2175</fpage><lpage>2180</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">6189571</pub-id></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="F1" position="float"><label>Fig. (1).</label><caption><p>Cell growth of Ca9-22 cells. RA-treated (red line) and untreated (blue line) Ca9-22 cells. Ca9-22 cells were treated with 10-<sup>6</sup>M of <italic>9-cis </italic>RA. <italic>Ordinate</italic>: cell number; <italic>abscissa</italic>: days after cells were seeded, with or without <italic>9-cis</italic> RA. The experiments were repeated twice, and a representative result is shown.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TODENTJ-2-78_F1"/></fig><fig id="F2" position="float"><label>Fig. (2).</label><caption><p>Expressions of 14 RA-up-regulated genes. <italic>Ordinate</italic>, relative expression level; <italic>abscissa</italic>, days after Ca9-22 cells were seeded with (red line) or without (blue line) 9-cis RA. See Results for explanations of gene types &#x02018;A&#x02019;, &#x02018;B&#x02019; and &#x02018;C.&#x02019; The expression of each gene was examined, at least, twice; representative results are demonstrated.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TODENTJ-2-78_F2"/></fig><fig id="F3" position="float"><label>Fig. (3).</label><caption><p>Expressions of 17 RA-down-regulated genes. Explanatory notes as in Fig. (<bold><xref ref-type="fig" rid="F1">2</xref></bold>). The expression of each gene was examined, at least, twice; representative results are demonstrated</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TODENTJ-2-78_F3"/></fig><fig id="F4" position="float"><label>Fig. (4).</label><caption><p>Expression of CBR3 mRNA in cells and tissues. CBR3 mRNA was determined by QRT-PCR. <italic>Ordinate</italic>, relative expression level: <italic>abscissa</italic>, samples from OSCC (gray bars), severe dysplasia (yellow bars), moderate dysplasia (green bars), mild dysplasia (pink bars) and hyperplasia (red bar).</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TODENTJ-2-78_F4"/></fig><fig id="F5" position="float"><label>Fig. (5).</label><caption><p>Expression of CBR3 protein in Ca9-22 cells treated with RA or transfected with <italic>CBR3</italic> expression vectors.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TODENTJ-2-78_F5"/></fig><fig id="F6" position="float"><label>Fig (6)</label><caption><p>Cell growth of <italic>CBR3</italic>-transfected and vector-transfected Ca9-22 cells. <italic>Ordinate</italic>, cell number; <italic>abscissa</italic>, days after cells were seeded. The experiments were repeated twice, and a representative result is shown.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TODENTJ-2-78_F6"/></fig><fig id="F7" position="float"><label>Fig (7)</label><caption><p>Cell mobility assay. Results for (A) CBR3-transfected and vector transfected or (B) <italic>9-cis</italic> RA-treated and untreated Ca9-22 cells.</p></caption><graphic xlink:href="TODENTJ-2-78_F7"/></fig><table-wrap id="T1" position="float"><label>Table 1.</label><caption><p>Effects of 9-cis-RA on the Gene Expression in Ca9-22 Cells.</p></caption><table frame="border" rules="all" width="100%"><thead><tr><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Up-Regulated Genes</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">GenBank No.</th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Fold Intensity<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="T1F1"><italic><sup>a</sup></italic></xref></th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Expression Type<xref ref-type="table-fn" rid="T1F2"><italic><sup>b</sup></italic></xref></th><th rowspan="1" colspan="1">Primer pairs</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Solute carrier family 37 (glycerol-3-phosphate transporter),  member 2 (SLC37A2)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">XM_166184.1</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.23&#x000b1;0.23</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">B</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">AGCCTCACCCTGCTCTCCTTCC /  CGGGCCTGCTTTTCTGCTTAAT</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ankyrin repeat and SOCS box-containing 2 (ASB2)</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">NM_016150.3</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">4.12&#x000b1;0.23</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">A</td><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1">CGCCCAGAGTGGACAGTTGGAG / GGCACCCTGTGACAGCAGAAAC</td></tr><tr><td align="center" rowspan="1" colspan="1